-- Q10 [Cell Cycle Control & Checkpoints | hard | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub10$Cell Cycle Control & Checkpoints$sub10$, $qt10$A geneticist identifies a single point mutation in the gene encoding a cell-cycle Cdk that locks the kinase in an active conformation, allowing it to phosphorylate its downstream targets even when no cyclin is bound. If a cell carrying this gain-of-function allele continues to divide, what is the MOST likely consequence for its passage through the cell-cycle checkpoints?$qt10$, $op10$[{"label": "A", "text": "The cell cycle slows and arrests, because a cyclin-independent Cdk now behaves as a tumor suppressor that brakes division."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The cell pushes past a checkpoint before all required conditions are met, accelerating cell-cycle progression as the proto-oncogene becomes an oncogene."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The mutation has no functional effect, because a Cdk must still be dephosphorylated by cyclin before it can phosphorylate downstream targets."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The defect is corrected at the G1 checkpoint by p53, which detects the abnormal Cdk protein and triggers apoptosis before any uncontrolled division occurs."}]$op10$::jsonb, 'B', $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is B because a Cdk that becomes active without binding cyclin is the textbook example of a positive regulator whose gain-of-function mutation converts a proto-oncogene into an oncogene. The text states that "a mutation that allows Cdk to be activated without being partnered with cyclin could push the cell cycle past a checkpoint before all of the required conditions are met," and defines an oncogene as "any gene that, when altered, leads to an increase in the rate of cell-cycle progression" (lines 13072-13073, 13080-13081). Because Cdks are the positive regulators that drive forward momentum, increasing their activity speeds the cycle rather than stopping it; this is the opposite of a tumor-suppressor loss-of-function, which removes a brake rather than jamming the accelerator. (Choice A) is reversed: the activated Cdk is a positive regulator (a proto-oncogene), not a tumor suppressor, and tumor suppressors such as Rb, p53, and p21 are the brake-like proteins "that, when activated, can prevent the cell from undergoing uncontrolled division" (lines 13085-13086). (Choice B) is correct because the unregulated Cdk drives the cell past a checkpoint prematurely, increasing the rate of cell-cycle progression. (Choice C) inverts the activation requirements: the text says a Cdk becomes "fully activated" by binding cyclin and then being "phosphorylated by another kinase" (lines 12960-12961), so cyclin binding and activating phosphorylation promote Cdk activity, and dephosphorylation by cyclin is not a real step. (Choice D) is a partial truth, because p53 does act at the G1 checkpoint and can trigger apoptosis, but it "acts when there is damaged DNA" and monitors genomic damage and cell stress (lines 12992-12994), not the activation state of an individual Cdk protein, so it would not specifically catch this lesion. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting a cellular outcome from a described mutation by integrating Cdk activation requirements with the proto-oncogene-to-oncogene mechanism rather than recalling a single definition.$ex10$, 'hard', '2C', $cc10$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
