-- Q18 [Mitosis Phases | medium | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub18$Mitosis Phases$sub18$, $qt18$A researcher treats a population of cultured human cells with a drug that completely blocks the degradation of cohesin proteins, leaving spindle assembly, kinetochore attachment, and chromosome condensation otherwise intact. As the treated cells progress through mitosis, at which point do they most likely arrest, and in what chromosomal configuration?$qt18$, $op18$[{"label": "A", "text": "In metaphase, with the chromosomes aligned at the metaphase plate and maximally condensed, because sister chromatids cannot separate until cohesin degrades."}, {"label": "B", "text": "In anaphase, with the separated chromosomes stalled partway to the poles, because the spindle can no longer pull the chromatids the remaining distance."}, {"label": "C", "text": "In prophase, with the chromosomes condensing, because the nuclear envelope cannot finish dissociating into small vesicles."}, {"label": "D", "text": "At the end of telophase, with two complete daughter nuclei that stay joined, because the cell cannot pinch its cytoplasm apart."}]$op18$::jsonb, 'A', $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is A because anaphase cannot begin until the cohesin holding sister chromatids together is degraded, so a cell whose cohesin can never degrade completes every earlier step but stalls at the metaphase-to-anaphase transition. By metaphase the chromosomes have already aligned at the metaphase plate and reached their most condensed state while \"still tightly attached to each other by cohesin proteins... the chromosomes are maximally condensed\" (line 12664), and anaphase only proceeds when \"the cohesin proteins degrade, and the sister chromatids separate at the centromere\" (line 12666). With cohesin degradation blocked, the cell remains arrested in metaphase with aligned, maximally condensed chromosomes whose sister chromatids stay fused. (Choice A) is correct because alignment finishes in metaphase but separation, which requires cohesin loss, cannot occur, freezing the cell in the aligned, maximally condensed metaphase state. (Choice B) reverses which step is blocked: cohesin must degrade before anaphase can even start (line 12666), so the cell never enters anaphase and chromatids never separate, rather than stalling mid-anaphase. (Choice C) confuses the timing, because dissociation of the nuclear envelope into vesicles is a prophase event (line 12628) unrelated to cohesin and is completed long before the cohesin-dependent step. (Choice D) describes an adjacent but distinct failure: keeping daughter cells joined reflects an inability of the actin contractile ring to complete cytokinesis (lines 12692-12695), not the chromatid-separation failure caused by stable cohesin, and the cell here never reaches telophase at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the precise arrest point of a perturbed system by tracing how blocking one molecular event propagates through the ordered phases of mitosis.$ex18$, 'medium', '2C', $cc18$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc18$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
