UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a19D$Reversing the all-must-be-attached rule into an all-must-be-detached rule, so a single unattached kinetochore is wrongly imagined to be ignored$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Mitosis Phases | hard | Skill 2 | ans D]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub20$Mitosis Phases$sub20$, $qt20$A geneticist labels every base pair of a diploid cell's DNA during the preceding S phase and then watches one full mitotic division proceed to completion. After anaphase delivers one member of each sister-chromatid pair to opposite poles and cytokinesis finishes, the geneticist asks how the chromosome content and genetic information of the two resulting daughter cells compare to each other and to the original parent cell.$qt20$, $op20$[{"label": "A", "text": "The daughter cells are genetically identical to each other but each is haploid, because separating the sister chromatids halves the cell's chromosome number relative to the parent."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The daughter cells are diploid but genetically distinct from each other, because the sister chromatids carried independently assorted alleles before they separated at the centromere."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The daughter cells are genetically identical and diploid, but their chromosomes stay maximally condensed within both new nuclei because condensation peaks during chromosome separation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The daughter cells are genetically identical to each other and to the parent and remain diploid, because each pole received one of the two identical sister chromatids of every chromosome."}]$op20$::jsonb, 'D', $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is D because the sister chromatids separated in anaphase are identical copies made during S phase, so distributing one chromatid of every pair to each pole gives each daughter nucleus a complete, identical, diploid chromosome set. The text states the cell cycle 'ultimately produces two identical (clone) cells' (line 12521) and that mitosis followed by cytokinesis results 'in two genetically identical daughter cells' (lines 12533-12534), while S-phase replication forms 'identical pairs of DNA molecules—sister chromatids' (lines 12552-12553). Because each chromatid is a full chromosome rather than a half, no reduction in ploidy occurs; the daughters match the parent and each other. (Choice A) is wrong because sister-chromatid separation does not halve ploidy—each separated chromatid is a complete chromosome, so the daughters remain diploid; reduction to haploid is a meiosis outcome, not mitosis. (Choice B) is wrong because the sister chromatids are identical S-phase copies, making the daughters identical rather than distinct, and independent assortment of alleles is a meiotic event that does not occur here. (Choice C) is wrong because chromosomes 'begin to decondense (unravel)' in telophase, relaxing 'into a stretched-out chromatin configuration' (lines 12671-12672), so the new daughter nuclei contain decondensed chromatin, not maximally condensed chromosomes—condensation peaks at metaphase (lines 12485-12486). (Choice D) is correct because each pole receives one identical sister chromatid of every chromosome, yielding two genetically identical diploid cells. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the identity of S-phase sister chromatids to the anaphase distribution mechanism in order to predict the genetic and ploidy outcome of the daughter cells.$ex20$, 'hard', '2C', $cc20$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
