-- Q21 [Mitotic Structures | easy | Skill 1 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub21$Mitotic Structures$sub21$, $qt21$A researcher images a cell in prometaphase and labels the single protein structure on each sister chromatid where a spindle microtubule physically docks. To which structure is the researcher pointing?$qt21$, $op21$[{"label": "A", "text": "The kinetochore, the protein structure that assembles at the centromere of each sister chromatid and binds the spindle microtubules"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The centriole, the rod-like body inside the centrosome that the spindle microtubules attach to"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The cohesin ring, the protein complex along the chromatid arms that the spindle microtubules attach to"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cleavage furrow, the actin-based site where the spindle microtubules attach to pull the chromosome"}]$op21$::jsonb, 'A', $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is A because the kinetochore is the protein structure that forms at the centromere of each sister chromatid and serves as the docking site for spindle microtubules. The text states that during prometaphase "Each sister chromatid develops a protein structure called a kinetochore in its centromeric region" and that "The proteins of the kinetochore attract and bind to the mitotic spindle microtubules," so a microtubule physically docks on a chromatid at its kinetochore. The other named structures exist in dividing cells but are not the microtubule-binding site on the chromatid. (Choice A) Correct: the kinetochore assembles at the centromere of each chromatid and is the binding site for spindle microtubules. (Choice B) Incorrect: centrioles are the "rod-like objects" inside the centrosome from which microtubules emanate at the poles, not the docking site on the chromatid. (Choice C) Incorrect: cohesin proteins "bind to each other" the sister chromatids, holding them together rather than binding spindle microtubules. (Choice D) Incorrect: the cleavage furrow is the actin-ring fissure of cytokinesis, an event after chromosome segregation, not a microtubule attachment site. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the defined function and location of the kinetochore as the spindle-microtubule attachment structure.$ex21$, 'easy', '2C', $cc21$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 70
  ) RETURNING id
)
