-- Q22 [Mitotic Structures | medium | Skill 2 | ans B]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub22$Mitotic Structures$sub22$, $qt22$A cell biologist uses a targeted technique to depolymerize only the astral microtubules of a dividing animal cell, leaving the kinetochore microtubules and the interpolar (polar) microtubules fully intact and functional. The biologist then predicts which aspect of mitosis is MOST directly disrupted in the treated cell. The most defensible prediction is that:$qt22$, $op22$[{"label": "A", "text": "sister chromatids can no longer attach to the spindle, because astral microtubules are the fibers that bind the kinetochores"}, {"label": "B", "text": "positioning and orientation of the spindle within the cell are impaired, because astral microtubules near the poles aid in spindle orientation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "the two centrosomes can no longer separate to opposite poles, because astral microtubules are the fibers that push the poles apart from the cell's center"}, {"label": "D", "text": "chromosomes can no longer align at the metaphase plate, because astral microtubules are the fibers that congress chromosomes to the equator"}]$op22$::jsonb, 'B', $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is B because astral microtubules are the specific spindle subset that radiates from the poles to orient and anchor the spindle, so removing only them impairs spindle positioning while leaving chromosome capture and segregation machinery intact. The text states that "Astral microtubules are located near the poles, aid in spindle orientation, and are required for the regulation of mitosis," which assigns the orientation/anchoring role uniquely to this subset. Because the kinetochore microtubules (which bind chromosomes) and the polar microtubules (which drive elongation) are left untouched in the scenario, chromosome attachment, congression, and centrosome separation can still proceed; only the astral-dependent function—orienting the spindle relative to the cell—is selectively lost. The prediction therefore isolates the single function the eliminated fibers perform. (Choice A) is wrong because it is the kinetochore microtubules, not the astral subset, that bind the kinetochores—"some of these microtubules come into contact with and firmly bind to the kinetochores"—and those fibers remain intact here. (Choice B) is correct because astral microtubules near the poles "aid in spindle orientation," so their selective loss most directly impairs how the spindle is positioned within the cell. (Choice C) is wrong because separating the two centrosomes toward opposite poles is driven by the microtubules that "extend between the centrosomes, pushing them farther apart," not by the outward-radiating astral fibers. (Choice D) is wrong because aligning chromosomes at the metaphase plate depends on kinetochore-microtubule attachment that orients each chromosome, "until the kinetochores of sister chromatids face the opposite poles," not on astral microtubules. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the specific functional consequence of selectively perturbing one microtubule subset while reasoning that the intact subsets preserve the other mitotic functions.$ex22$, 'medium', '2C', $cc22$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
