-- Q25 [Mitotic Structures | hard | Skill 2 | ans A]
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'Reproduction', $sub25$Mitotic Structures$sub25$, $qt25$A cell in anaphase is filmed under a microscope, and the observer notes that its overall shape stretches from roughly spherical into an elongated oval even as each separated chromosome moves toward a pole. Which subset of spindle microtubules is MOST directly responsible for this lengthening of the whole cell?$qt25$, $op25$[{"label": "A", "text": "The polar microtubules, which do not engage chromosomes but overlap one another midway between the poles and slide apart to elongate the cell"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The kinetochore microtubules, which bind the sister chromatids and slide past one another at the equator to elongate the cell"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The astral microtubules, which radiate from the poles and slide past one another at the equator to elongate the cell"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The cohesin fibers, which span the metaphase plate and lengthen to push the two poles farther apart"}]$op25$::jsonb, 'A', $ex25$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is A because the polar microtubules are the only spindle class that overlaps midway between the poles and slides against itself to drive whole-cell elongation. The text defines that "Spindle microtubules that do not engage the chromosomes are called polar microtubules. These microtubules overlap each other midway between the two poles and contribute to cell elongation," and the anaphase description states the "cell becomes visibly elongated (oval shaped) as the polar microtubules slide against each other at the metaphase plate where they overlap." Kinetochore microtubules instead bind chromosomes and pull them poleward, while astral microtubules sit near the poles and orient the spindle rather than pushing the poles apart; cohesin is a protein that holds sister chromatids together and degrades in anaphase, not a force-generating fiber. (Choice A) Correct: polar microtubules overlap at the cell's middle and slide apart, lengthening the cell into an oval. (Choice B) Wrong: kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes via the kinetochore and pull chromatids toward the poles; they do not overlap and slide at the equator to elongate the cell. (Choice C) Wrong: astral microtubules are located near the poles and aid in spindle orientation and the regulation of mitosis, not in equatorial sliding that elongates the cell. (Choice D) Wrong: cohesin is a protein complex that binds sister chromatids together and degrades at anaphase onset; it is not a microtubule and does not push the poles apart. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because the test-taker must connect an observed phenotype (the cell becoming oval) to the specific mechanistic subset of microtubules that generates it, distinguishing among three structurally similar spindle populations rather than recalling a single definition.$ex25$, 'hard', '2C', $cc25$Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)$cc25$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
